BT /F1 10 Tf 50 800 Td 13 TL (Kestrel Valley University, Department of Mechanical Engineering) Tj T* (MEng Mechanical Engineering, Year 2. Module ME2104 Solid Mechanics Laboratory) Tj T* (Lab report 3: Young's modulus of an aluminium alloy by cantilever deflection) Tj T* (Lab group C7 \(4 students\). This report is my individual write-up. Date of experiment: 21) Tj T* (October 2025.) Tj T* () Tj T* (1. Aim) Tj T* () Tj T* (To measure the Young's modulus \(E\) of a 6082 aluminium alloy bar by loading it as a cantilever) Tj T* (and measuring the tip deflection, and to compare the result with the reference value of 69 GPa) Tj T* (given in the module data sheet.) Tj T* () Tj T* (2. Theory) Tj T* () Tj T* (For a cantilever of length L with a point load F at the free end, simple beam theory gives the) Tj T* (tip deflection as delta = F L^3 / \(3 E I\), where I is the second moment of area. For a) Tj T* (rectangular section of width b and thickness h, I = b h^3 / 12. A plot of deflection against) Tj T* (load should be a straight line through the origin with gradient L^3 / \(3 E I\), so E can be) Tj T* (found from the gradient.) Tj T* () Tj T* (3. Apparatus and method) Tj T* () Tj T* (The bar was clamped in a rigid bench vice with a free length of 300 mm, measured with a steel) Tj T* (rule \(resolution 1 mm\). Its width \(25.0 mm\) and thickness \(3.00 mm\) were measured with a) Tj T* (digital micrometer at five points along the bar and averaged. Tip deflection was read from a) Tj T* (dial gauge with a resolution of 0.01 mm, placed 5 mm in from the free end.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Loads were added from 0 N to 5.0 N in steps of 0.5 N using slotted masses on a hanger, then) Tj T* (removed in the same steps, so each run gave 11 loading and 11 unloading readings. The group did) Tj T* (three runs, re-zeroing the dial gauge before each one. Before the first run the bar was loaded) Tj T* (to 5.0 N and unloaded twice to settle it in the clamp.) Tj T* () Tj T* (4. Results) Tj T* () Tj T* (Table 1: Mean tip deflection over three runs \(loading\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (Load \(N\)    Deflection \(mm\)) Tj T* (0.0         0.00) Tj T* (1.0         2.41) Tj T* (2.0         4.79) Tj T* (3.0         7.21) Tj T* (4.0         9.62) Tj T* (5.0         12.00) Tj T* () Tj T* (\(Readings at the half-newton steps are in Appendix A.\)) Tj T* () Tj T* (A least-squares straight line through all 33 loading readings gives a gradient of 2.40 mm/N) Tj T* (with an intercept of 0.01 mm and R^2 = 0.9996. The unloading readings were on average 0.04 mm) Tj T* (higher than the loading readings at the same load, a small hysteresis that most likely comes) Tj T* (from slip in the clamp rather than from the material.) Tj T* () Tj T* (With I = 25.0 x 3.00^3 / 12 = 56.25 mm^4 and L = 300 mm:) Tj T* () Tj T* (E = L^3 / \(3 I x gradient\) = 27,000,000 / \(3 x 56.25 x 2.40\) = 66,700 N/mm^2 = 66.7 GPa.) Tj T* () Tj T* (5. Error analysis) Tj T* () Tj T* (The uncertainty in E was found by combining the fractional uncertainties of each measured) Tj T* ET
